#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166494 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "544f5154-5213-5898-a45d-4546bc8004c8" :knowledge-point/title "Understanding the Periodic Extension of a Function"}] :question/id #uuid "5888d4ce-bc45-540b-afa0-901008abfcde" :question/math-academy-id "e-21787" :question/problem "Let $F(x) = \\cos(2x)$ for $x\\in\\mathbb R.$ Suppose $f(x)$ is the $\\pi$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ from $x \\in [0,\\pi)$ to $x \\in \\mathbb{R}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x+\\pi) = f(x)$ for all $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$\n\n- $f(x) = F(x)$ for all $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$\n\n- $f\\left(x+\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right) = f(x)$ for all $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$" :question/worked-solution "Since $f$ is the $\\pi$-periodic extension of $F,$ we have  \n\n- $f(x+\\pi)=f(x)$ for all $x\\in\\mathbb{R},$ and\n\n- $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in[0,\\pi).$\n\nWith that in mind, we examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. This is exactly the definition of $\\pi$-periodicity:\n\n$$\nf(x+\\pi)=f(x)\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in\\mathbb{R}.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Notice that $F$ itself is $\\pi$-periodic, because for any $x\\in\\mathbb{R},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x+\\pi )=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(x+\\pi ))=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x+2\\pi )=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)=F(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $F$ already satisfies the periodicity rule, the $\\pi$-periodic extension agrees with $F$ everywhere, so $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in\\mathbb{R}.$\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, take $x=0.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})=f(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=F(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )=-1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x)=f(0)=F(0)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f\\left(x+\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right) \\neq f(x)$ for $x=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166495 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fadf4f64-3569-56b2-98e1-f6681892054a" :knowledge-point/title "Checking Orthogonality of Sines and Cosines"}] :question/id #uuid "052f11a0-2f27-5285-83af-6d8fdb729024" :question/math-academy-id "e-21788" :question/problem "$$\n\\langle f(x),g(x) \\rangle = \\displaystyle\\int_{0}^{2\\pi} f(x) g(x) \\, \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nGiven the inner product on the set of continuous functions over $x\\in [0,2\\pi)$ defined above, compute $\\displaystyle \\langle \\sin(6x), \\cos(4x) \\rangle$ and determine if functions $f(x)=\\sin(6x)$ and $g(x)=\\cos(4x)$ are orthogonal with respect to the inner product." :question/worked-solution "We begin by using the product-to-sum identity\n\n$$\n\\sin(6x)\\cos(4x)\n=\\dfrac{1}{2}\\big(\\sin(10x)+\\sin(2x)\\big).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(10x)+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{20}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(10x)-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)]}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{20}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(20\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0))-\\frac{1}{4}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{20}(1-1)-\\frac{1}{4}(1-1) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)=\\sin(6x)$ and $g(x)=\\cos(4x)$ \n$\\text{are orthogonal}$ with respect to the inner product."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166496 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "304c015d-cbb6-5989-a102-d6f0f5f38604" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Expressions For Fourier Coefficients"}] :question/id #uuid "d2127a8e-14bb-5574-a542-852333fe4cca" :question/math-academy-id "e-21789" :question/problem "Let $F(x) = 2x^2+5$ for $x\\in\\mathbb R,$ and let $f(x)$ be the $2\\pi$-periodic extension of $F$ from $x\\in [0,2\\pi)$ to $x\\in \\mathbb R.$ The Fourier series of $f$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x) \\sim \\dfrac{a_0}{2} + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\left(a_n \\cos(nx) + b_n \\sin(nx)\\right).\n$$\n\nDetermine the integral expression for the coefficient $a_4.$" :question/worked-solution "The usual inner product of functions $f$ and $g$ over $x\\in [0,2\\pi)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\langle f(x),g(x) \\rangle = \\int_{0}^{2\\pi} f(x)g(x)\\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nFor a $2\\pi$-periodic function $f(x)$ that is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,2\\pi),$ the Fourier series of $f$ is given by \n\n$$\nf(x) \\sim \\dfrac{a_0}{2}+\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\\left( a_n\\cos(nx)+b_n\\sin(nx)\\right),\n$$\n\nwhere $a_0,$ $a_n,$ and $b_n$ are constants to be determined. \n\nNow, we find the inner product of $f(x)$ with $\\cos(4x){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩ & = \\\\ ⟨\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩ & = \\\\ ⟨\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2},\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }⟨{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }⟨{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩ & = \\\\ \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}⟨1,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the functions\n$1, \\sin(x), \\cos(x), \\sin(2x), \\cos(2x), \\ldots$\nare orthogonal with respect to the defined inner product, the only nonzero term in the final expression above is\n\n$$\na_4 \\left\\langle \\cos(4x), \\cos(4x) \\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{1}{16}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8x)]}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2\\pi -0)-\\frac{1}{16}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(16\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩ & ={a}_{4}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩ \\\\ {a}_{4} & =\\frac{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩}{⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩} \\\\ {a}_{4} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(2{x}^{2}+5)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167203 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "544f5154-5213-5898-a45d-4546bc8004c8" :knowledge-point/title "Understanding the Periodic Extension of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9ba16a9d-5fbd-527f-8383-e8b7dba28967" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164737} {:answer/id #uuid "b0622973-b233-5a42-83f3-94fbfa45cbf1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164738} {:answer/id #uuid "1bc86564-2e12-5b94-a1c4-41465a065167" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164739} {:answer/id #uuid "26f06f13-1531-5eff-b716-5113a6063051" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164740} {:answer/id #uuid "52a29d9c-c2dd-5ff6-a68b-8fe9f7bb49e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164741}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b0622973-b233-5a42-83f3-94fbfa45cbf1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164738} :answer-field/id #uuid "36d3814c-d28f-5b80-99c6-6dd8a507a264" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164736}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "97cef292-5a85-5947-928d-d07517a20540" :question/math-academy-id "q-338489" :question/problem "Let $F(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$ for $x\\in R.$ Suppose $f(x)$ is the $\\pi $-periodic extension of $F(x)$ from $x\\in [0,\\pi )$ to $x\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x+\\pi )=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R$\n- $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in R$\n- $f(x+\\frac{5\\pi }{2})=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R$" :question/worked-solution "Since $f$ is the $\\pi $-periodic extension of $F,$ we have \n\n- $f(x+\\pi )=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R,$ and\n\n- $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in [0,\\pi ).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. This is exactly the definition of $\\pi $-periodicity: \n\n$$\nf(x+\\pi )=f(x)\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in R.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Notice that $F$ itself is $\\pi $-periodic, because for any $x\\in R,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x+\\pi )=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(x+\\pi ))=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+2\\pi )=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)=F(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $F$ already satisfies the periodicity rule, the $\\pi $-periodic extension agrees with $F$ everywhere, so $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in R.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $f$ is $\\pi $-periodic, shifting by $2\\pi $ does not change the value of $f,$ so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x+\\frac{5\\pi }{2})=f((x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})+2\\pi )=f(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBut $f(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ does not always equal $f(x).$ For example, take $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{\\pi }{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{2})=f(\\frac{5\\pi }{6})=F(\\frac{5\\pi }{6})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{6})=\\frac{1}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{\\pi }{3})=F(\\frac{\\pi }{3})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x+\\frac{5\\pi }{2})\\ne f(x)$ for $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186330762 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "544f5154-5213-5898-a45d-4546bc8004c8" :knowledge-point/title "Understanding the Periodic Extension of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6a7f85b6-60c4-5eba-b385-bdf81696351f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330764} {:answer/id #uuid "f3b8be5d-9e4f-51d1-8742-d8ccd443a738" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330765} {:answer/id #uuid "6028ebb3-e2c4-5318-b4e9-257f1bfd4db7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330766} {:answer/id #uuid "864391c2-939e-58e8-b3f2-68ceb9e4020c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330767} {:answer/id #uuid "1456c26d-96c1-5749-b4e0-add5bd4720a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330768}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f3b8be5d-9e4f-51d1-8742-d8ccd443a738" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330765} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e5c3d88-768c-5c39-b020-1400a20a6a01" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330763}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "238cb61e-c331-5c28-af91-45990df477fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-338490" :question/problem "Let $F(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)$ for $x\\in R.$ Suppose $f(x)$ is the $\\pi $-periodic extension of $F(x)$ from $x\\in [0,\\pi )$ to $x\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x+\\pi )=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R$\n- $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in R$\n- $f(x)=f(x-2\\pi )$ for all $x\\in R$" :question/worked-solution "Since $f$ is the $\\pi $-periodic extension of $F,$ we have  \n\n- $f(x+\\pi )=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R,$ and\n\n- $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in [0,\\pi ).$\n\nWith that in mind, we examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. This is exactly the definition of $\\pi $-periodicity: \n\n$$\nf(x+\\pi )=f(x)\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in R.$\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, take $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})=f(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\pi )=f(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=F(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x)\\ne F(x)$ for $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$\n\n- Statement III is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =f((x-\\pi )+\\pi ) \\\\  & =f(x-\\pi ) \\\\  & =f((x-2\\pi )+\\pi ) \\\\  & =f(x-2\\pi )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186330769 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fadf4f64-3569-56b2-98e1-f6681892054a" :knowledge-point/title "Checking Orthogonality of Sines and Cosines"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bbd0ffc2-c8e3-529d-8049-5d3638620b0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330771} {:answer/id #uuid "d26ac4fd-1ffd-578f-a9d4-5c909f48ae1b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330772} {:answer/id #uuid "0449c6ea-00e6-5a36-a00a-2c2b42059ce7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330773} {:answer/id #uuid "ad88fdd9-cffd-562c-bca1-2cc94075e5fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330774}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0449c6ea-00e6-5a36-a00a-2c2b42059ce7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330773} :answer-field/id #uuid "d8e2a251-46b0-5cf8-a475-6272bb2966ca" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330770} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "776be329-8f23-508c-8dd8-7bf182679751" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "are orthogonal" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330776} {:answer/id #uuid "6b6d9eba-77ba-58b6-ac64-b323a8231ef3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "are not orthogonal" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330777}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "776be329-8f23-508c-8dd8-7bf182679751" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "are orthogonal" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330776} :answer-field/id #uuid "568a04da-4d23-5c8a-99b3-626f9f040ba5" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330775}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "737d0926-8221-571a-b8ab-6bf67dc652e8" :question/math-academy-id "q-340369" :question/problem "$$\n⟨f(x),g(x)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(x)g(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nGiven the inner product on the set of continuous functions over $x\\in [0,2\\pi )$ defined above, select the correct options below.\n$\\,$\n$⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)⟩=$\n{{field-1}}\n\n$\\,$\nThe functions $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$ and $g(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)$ \n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "We begin by using the product-to-sum identity\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)=\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7x)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7x)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{14}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7x)+\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)]}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{14}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(14\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0))+\\frac{1}{6}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{14}(1-1)+\\frac{1}{6}(1-1) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$ and $g(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)$ $\\text{are orthogonal}$ with respect to the inner product."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167220 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "304c015d-cbb6-5989-a102-d6f0f5f38604" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Expressions For Fourier Coefficients"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8664bf48-b5d4-5d30-9acf-912dfc8268a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(⟨f(x), \\cos (5x)⟩)/(⟨\\sin (5x), \\sin (5x)⟩)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164869} {:answer/id #uuid "c67cd987-6ec5-57a7-9c7c-effb0b0eca94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(⟨f(x), \\cos (5x)⟩)/(⟨\\cos (5x), \\cos (5x)⟩)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164870} {:answer/id #uuid "55adcd35-c626-5f20-b50a-cf74b96126f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(⟨f(x), \\sin (5x)⟩)/(⟨\\sin (5x), \\sin (5x)⟩)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164871} {:answer/id #uuid "6561e55b-1285-52d8-97de-2927a802d5a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(⟨\\sin (5x), \\sin (5x)⟩)/(⟨f(x), \\cos (5x)⟩)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164872} {:answer/id #uuid "674d6ba9-cecb-5505-ae5e-615d1b2906c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(⟨\\cos (5x), \\cos (5x)⟩)/(⟨f(x), \\cos (5x)⟩)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164873}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c67cd987-6ec5-57a7-9c7c-effb0b0eca94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(⟨f(x), \\cos (5x)⟩)/(⟨\\cos (5x), \\cos (5x)⟩)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164870} :answer-field/id #uuid "9c7846c0-b2b6-5038-9ca3-dc4dfc0646cf" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164868} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9dc7ec21-5577-5a65-9979-79200f1a6b75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{0}^{∞}x^{2}\\cos (5x)dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164875} {:answer/id #uuid "e1bad02e-e232-53fc-8690-ea138de9d8ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{0}^{∞}x^{2}\\sin (5x)dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164876} {:answer/id #uuid "e4298d08-8ab3-57c9-bc2a-6a66b95cd618" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{0}^{2π}x^{2}\\sin (5x)dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164877} {:answer/id #uuid "e76aa029-6c75-563f-a800-3515bb7cd888" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{0}^{2π}x^{2}\\cos (5x)dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164878} {:answer/id #uuid "ed57003e-41b1-5afc-9a4c-a046bfd233ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{− π}^{π}x^{2}\\cos (5x)dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164879} {:answer/id #uuid "f7bfe1a2-18b2-57c6-ab93-12c6304c0ec5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{− π}^{π}x^{2}\\sin (5x)dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164880}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ed57003e-41b1-5afc-9a4c-a046bfd233ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{− π}^{π}x^{2}\\cos (5x)dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164879} :answer-field/id #uuid "5382fbf0-4b1a-56a6-9249-8660943fa491" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164874} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f85944a-3739-5bd3-9b9f-31e1f837c946" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1)/(2π)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164882} {:answer/id #uuid "30729cbd-5e16-5d82-9c54-9c174f94c35b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(π)/(5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164883} {:answer/id #uuid "5a1cc985-9f7d-53d0-b9b2-8e123719ef83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1)/(π)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164884} {:answer/id #uuid "60006be5-37c7-5639-af46-811b8ab5e7b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1)/(5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164885} {:answer/id #uuid "6553a0b5-752e-5ac2-a4cc-fbb7b0d5719b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2)/(π)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164886}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5a1cc985-9f7d-53d0-b9b2-8e123719ef83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1)/(π)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164884} :answer-field/id #uuid "271d24b0-f894-5072-8a47-b0f5a9fb104a" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164881}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "aa20d8e3-c4d9-5112-bd9c-ec30f6aaa6fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-340418" :question/problem "Let $F(x)={x}^{2}$ for $x\\in R,$ and let $f(x)$ be the $2\\pi $-periodic extension of $F$ from $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi )$ to $x\\in R.$ The Fourier series of $f$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the expression for the coefficient ${a}_{5}$ below, where $⟨\\,,⟩$ denotes the usual inner product on functions over $[-\\pi ,\\pi ).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{5} & ={{field-1}} \\\\  & ={{field-2}}\\,\\cdot \\,{{field-3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The usual inner product of functions $f$ and $g$ over $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi )$ is given by\n\n$$\n⟨f(x),g(x)⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)g(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFor a $2\\pi $-periodic function $f(x)$ that's piecewise continuous over $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ),$ the Fourier series of $f$ is given by \n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)),\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{0},$ ${a}_{n},$ and ${b}_{n}$ are constants to be determined. \n\nNow, we find the inner product of $f(x)$ with $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)⟩ & = \\\\ ⟨\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)⟩ & = \\\\ ⟨\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2},\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }⟨{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }⟨{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)⟩ & = \\\\ \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}⟨1,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the functions\n$1,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x),…$\nare orthogonal with respect to the defined inner product, the only nonzero term in the final expression above is\n\n$$\n{a}_{5}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)⟩.\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)⟩ & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(10x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{1}{20}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(10x)]}_{-\\pi }^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\pi -(-\\pi ))-\\frac{1}{20}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(10\\pi )-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-10\\pi ))) \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)⟩ & ={a}_{5}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)⟩ \\\\ {a}_{5} & =\\frac{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)⟩}{⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)⟩} \\\\ {a}_{5} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\cdot {\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186330778 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "544f5154-5213-5898-a45d-4546bc8004c8" :knowledge-point/title "Understanding the Periodic Extension of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "61d142cc-e17c-5323-a3f5-444bbea74b94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330780} {:answer/id #uuid "a22528d6-a9bc-52a9-a65a-11f2a13fb688" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330781} {:answer/id #uuid "2bfff76c-6f49-5425-97a8-6ed0aba0c519" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330782} {:answer/id #uuid "6d8c71c0-ac8d-51aa-ab1c-850eccdf1e39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330783} {:answer/id #uuid "2d673da4-6b79-525c-9beb-81a138c3a4e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330784}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2d673da4-6b79-525c-9beb-81a138c3a4e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330784} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b118759-5210-526c-a6c2-c0a1dddaa979" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330779}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5dd485b5-3439-54f6-ac7d-a6da6d6627be" :question/math-academy-id "q-340510" :question/problem "Let $F(x)=x$ for $x\\in R.$ Suppose $f(x)$ is the $3$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ from $x\\in [0,3)$ to $x\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x+6)=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R$\n- $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in [0,3)$\n- $f(x+1)=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R$" :question/worked-solution "Since $f$ is the $3$-periodic extension of $F,$ we have \n\n- $f(x+3)=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R,$ and\n\n- $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in [0,3).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x+6)=f((x+3)+3)=f(x+3)=f(x),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in R.$\n\n- Statement II is true by definition of the periodic extension.\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, take $x=2.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2+1)=f(3)=F(3)=3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2)=F(2)=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186330785 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fadf4f64-3569-56b2-98e1-f6681892054a" :knowledge-point/title "Checking Orthogonality of Sines and Cosines"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ef40c4e6-0f35-5d77-a60d-899e3b96f1c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330787} {:answer/id #uuid "0b76771f-1840-5af5-ba67-5e0658e67e3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330788} {:answer/id #uuid "79c4f95e-0e47-5c16-b7bf-c8f8a640761e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330789} {:answer/id #uuid "83ea66ed-b584-586d-80d6-834ece8dc7c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330790}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "79c4f95e-0e47-5c16-b7bf-c8f8a640761e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330789} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e02ae9b-ddf6-57fa-8d8a-5f3afa592de3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330786} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c56f765a-f5ed-5eba-b7d1-8305e6c8ff43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "are orthogonal" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330792} {:answer/id #uuid "5b266eaa-eeaf-5842-a591-c98126d3bec3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "are not orthogonal" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330793}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c56f765a-f5ed-5eba-b7d1-8305e6c8ff43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "are orthogonal" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330792} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd57d2f3-bf0f-50ec-b608-3ac712ae3fd4" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330791}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0a425ff5-8d1c-54df-a9b1-2af91c0d28d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-340528" :question/problem "$$\n⟨f(x),g(x)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(x)g(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nGiven the inner product on the set of continuous functions over $x\\in [0,2\\pi ]$ defined above, select the correct options below.$\\,$\n$⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x),1⟩=$ \n{{field-1}}\n\n$\\,$\nThe functions $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)$ and $g(x)=1$ \n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Evaluating the inner product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x),1⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)]}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(0-0) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)$ and $g(x)=1$ \n$\\text{are orthogonal}$ with respect to the inner product."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186330794 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "304c015d-cbb6-5989-a102-d6f0f5f38604" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Expressions For Fourier Coefficients"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3fca65ab-d4f8-506c-8244-1454fdbec701" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)⟩}{⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)⟩}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330796} {:answer/id #uuid "991b13f2-9e76-526f-aa2b-e3606426ecaf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)⟩}{⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)⟩}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330797} {:answer/id #uuid "856435ed-a9a9-51e7-8f31-5fd2501a0185" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)⟩}{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)⟩}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330798} {:answer/id #uuid "51bac3f6-5d9f-5330-be87-38794b1793f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)⟩}{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)⟩}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330799} {:answer/id #uuid "4ac667d6-105f-5847-8bf8-a9d7de392b7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)⟩}{⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)⟩}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330800}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4ac667d6-105f-5847-8bf8-a9d7de392b7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)⟩}{⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)⟩}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330800} :answer-field/id #uuid "3cfad3e7-2cc7-5452-81ab-5b0a32a1663c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330795} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "052d408f-2b06-58a1-b2b4-70758d47badf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330802} {:answer/id #uuid "a7fd9ad1-ab53-53b5-bd45-1f1aaa4ed879" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330803} {:answer/id #uuid "ffddfcad-1641-5dc5-b967-d98bcf393289" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330804} {:answer/id #uuid "eea7a7cb-8d6f-56e8-beb3-41b3fbf14190" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330805} {:answer/id #uuid "c02e783c-d71a-5cb0-90d0-0b8b5fab8b89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330806}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eea7a7cb-8d6f-56e8-beb3-41b3fbf14190" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330805} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae4b00a3-3d89-514e-94eb-45cc7b170876" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330801} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39fff835-2744-517a-843f-52e8bc034927" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }({x}^{3}+5x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330808} {:answer/id #uuid "30adbaa9-3704-55c5-b06c-10ecac00be61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }({x}^{3}+5x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330809} {:answer/id #uuid "b0dd7cd5-29a7-51e8-afa7-cb1477eb350e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }({x}^{3}+5x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330810} {:answer/id #uuid "89719ccc-4bed-5797-a8d9-a6e4b81c85d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }({x}^{3}+5x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330811} {:answer/id #uuid "b9d7d981-c9af-5266-bf85-6336664e6597" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }({x}^{3}+5x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330812} {:answer/id #uuid "705a608a-5e19-514c-8b5a-608df1543bf8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }({x}^{3}+5x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330813}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b0dd7cd5-29a7-51e8-afa7-cb1477eb350e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }({x}^{3}+5x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330810} :answer-field/id #uuid "a4bb4dd8-e21b-5d8d-b4e2-fdfd452cd4c1" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330807}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "df849e8a-df1b-5b2f-bbc1-5b6e2ad9aeb2" :question/math-academy-id "q-340638" :question/problem "Let $F(x)={x}^{3}+5x$ for $x\\in R,$ and let $f(x)$ be the $2\\pi $-periodic extension of $F$ from $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi )$ to $x\\in R.$ The Fourier series of $f$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the expression for the coefficient ${a}_{6}$ below, where $⟨\\,,⟩$ denotes the usual inner product on functions over $[-\\pi ,\\pi ).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{6} & ={{field-1}} \\\\  & ={{field-2}}\\,\\cdot \\,{{field-3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The usual inner product of functions $f$ and $g$ over $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi )$ is given by\n\n$$\n⟨f(x),g(x)⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)g(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFor a $2\\pi $-periodic function $f(x)$ that is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ),$ the Fourier series of $f$ is given by \n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)),\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{0},$ ${a}_{n},$ and ${b}_{n}$ are constants to be determined. \n\nNow, we find the inner product of $f(x)$ with $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)⟩ & = \\\\ ⟨\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)⟩ & = \\\\ ⟨\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2},\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }⟨{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }⟨{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)⟩ & = \\\\ \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}⟨1,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the functions\n$1,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x),…$\nare orthogonal with respect to the defined inner product, the only nonzero term in the final expression above is\n\n$$\n{a}_{6}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)⟩.\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)⟩ & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡(6x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(12x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{1}{24}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12x)]}_{-\\pi }^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\pi -(-\\pi ))-\\frac{1}{24}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12\\pi )-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-12\\pi ))) \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)⟩ & ={a}_{6}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)⟩ \\\\ {a}_{6} & =\\frac{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)⟩}{⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)⟩} \\\\ {a}_{6} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\cdot {\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }({x}^{3}+5x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186330814 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "304c015d-cbb6-5989-a102-d6f0f5f38604" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Expressions For Fourier Coefficients"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "51afa5e1-db21-5f0c-b264-8237511bf486" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)⟩}{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)⟩}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330816} {:answer/id #uuid "ec6013c9-7e66-5134-8ad4-1e802761ad81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)⟩}{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)⟩}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330817} {:answer/id #uuid "183d18a3-a70e-5f8a-9079-3ff65f88b193" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)⟩}{⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)⟩}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330818} {:answer/id #uuid "1a11681c-9f02-5c7e-a11d-537b9bb86694" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)⟩}{⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)⟩}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330819} {:answer/id #uuid "18a6b8fb-fc71-569b-8c7b-f9ba32fa2389" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)⟩}{⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)⟩}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330820}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "18a6b8fb-fc71-569b-8c7b-f9ba32fa2389" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)⟩}{⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)⟩}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330820} :answer-field/id #uuid "f7b04e95-4ba2-5add-80fd-9032031ee849" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330815} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "51520b75-9b89-5237-adaa-515103ba70c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330822} {:answer/id #uuid "424efec3-c54e-5a56-97ae-e3d26a19e31f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330823} {:answer/id #uuid "722f6933-af8e-599e-a637-9bb426dfe6f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330824} {:answer/id #uuid "222dece3-3aea-5a1d-bdef-3758b86573ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330825} {:answer/id #uuid "9b5c59a3-97ab-5b96-8478-bae1b700616a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330826}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9b5c59a3-97ab-5b96-8478-bae1b700616a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330826} :answer-field/id #uuid "3aca8833-c3f8-5c4e-9487-fa6538a847c7" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330821} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23b450e6-2ad1-598c-b98f-3d104b02117f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330828} {:answer/id #uuid "e0194121-c133-5c87-82b2-71c65276b91f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330829} {:answer/id #uuid "01c37305-f58b-5f0e-8f01-c62256ef6546" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330830} {:answer/id #uuid "e1ad476b-415b-5551-a674-36128788df55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330831} {:answer/id #uuid "a5b7bcdf-6f73-515b-a674-06f62a2dc0b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330832} {:answer/id #uuid "cda21ba6-4e9a-59ff-a560-bae38fb214c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330833}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "01c37305-f58b-5f0e-8f01-c62256ef6546" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330830} :answer-field/id #uuid "19020950-662c-5f62-8f87-944f3110f05a" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330827}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8113289a-3226-5746-b1f3-c6dca42761fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-340647" :question/problem "Let $F(x)={x}^{2}$ for $x\\in R,$ and let $f(x)$ be the $2\\pi $-periodic extension of $F$ from $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi )$ to $x\\in R.$ The Fourier series of $f$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the expression for the coefficient ${b}_{2}$ below, where $⟨\\,,⟩$ denotes the usual inner product on functions over $[-\\pi ,\\pi ).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{2} & ={{field-1}} \\\\  & ={{field-2}}\\,\\cdot \\,{{field-3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The usual inner product of functions $f$ and $g$ over $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi )$ is given by\n\n$$\n⟨f(x),g(x)⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)g(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFor a $2\\pi $-periodic function $f(x)$ that is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ),$ the Fourier series of $f$ is given by \n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)),\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{0},$ ${a}_{n},$ and ${b}_{n}$ are constants to be determined. \n\nNow, we find the inner product of $f(x)$ with $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)⟩ & = \\\\ ⟨\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)⟩ & = \\\\ ⟨\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2},\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }⟨{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }⟨{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)⟩ & = \\\\ \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}⟨1,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the functions\n$1,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x),…$\nare orthogonal with respect to the defined inner product, the only nonzero term in the final expression above is\n\n$$\n{b}_{2}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)⟩.\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)⟩ & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{2}x-\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)]}_{-\\pi }^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\pi -(-\\pi ))-\\frac{1}{8}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\pi )-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-4\\pi ))) \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)⟩ & ={b}_{2}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)⟩ \\\\ {b}_{2} & =\\frac{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)⟩}{⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)⟩} \\\\ {b}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\cdot {\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186330834 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "304c015d-cbb6-5989-a102-d6f0f5f38604" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Expressions For Fourier Coefficients"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b77a79b-7ad5-5688-9168-07a38f1b36d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)⟩}{⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)⟩}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330836} {:answer/id #uuid "eb92b1bd-0eae-5f20-809e-221182636b99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)⟩}{⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)⟩}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330837} {:answer/id #uuid "9d5d7661-ca8f-5155-80c1-4f8a6b789161" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)⟩}{⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)⟩}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330838} {:answer/id #uuid "01c9c309-acdc-5319-a8a0-fca83f00a99a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)⟩}{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)⟩}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330839} {:answer/id #uuid "15433997-030c-52b0-ada0-4a3798577c3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)⟩}{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)⟩}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330840}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1b77a79b-7ad5-5688-9168-07a38f1b36d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)⟩}{⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)⟩}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330836} :answer-field/id #uuid "eb511cc0-743d-516e-8623-552b78f1033e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :a