id "5180702a-b667-532f-9806-8c59785acee6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }({x}^{2}+6x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330851} {:answer/id #uuid "8ff5c00b-10a6-5114-b54d-043d770f96e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }({x}^{2}+6x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330852} {:answer/id #uuid "52b05a3a-a24f-5413-8182-bfe9a49455cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }({x}^{2}+6x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330853}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5180702a-b667-532f-9806-8c59785acee6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }({x}^{2}+6x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330851} :answer-field/id #uuid "abfdea54-ba49-5b3e-8ec4-fe03d16ca16e" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330847}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7dc09a0e-31fd-55ae-8496-e8a1f65e9cde" :question/math-academy-id "q-340648" :question/problem "Let $F(x)={x}^{2}+6x$ for $x\\in R,$ and let $f(x)$ be the $2\\pi $-periodic extension of $F$ from $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi )$ to $x\\in R.$ The Fourier series of $f$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the expression for the coefficient ${b}_{3}$ below, where $⟨\\,,⟩$ denotes the usual inner product on functions over $[-\\pi ,\\pi ).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{3} & ={{field-1}} \\\\  & ={{field-2}}\\,\\cdot \\,{{field-3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The usual inner product of functions $f$ and $g$ over $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi )$ is given by\n\n$$\n⟨f(x),g(x)⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)g(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFor a $2\\pi $-periodic function $f(x)$ that's piecewise continuous over $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ),$ the Fourier series of $f$ is given by \n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)),\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{0},$ ${a}_{n},$ and ${b}_{n}$ are constants to be determined. \n\nNow, we find the inner product of $f(x)$ with $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)⟩ & = \\\\ ⟨\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)⟩ & = \\\\ ⟨\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2},\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }⟨{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }⟨{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)⟩ & = \\\\ \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}⟨1,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the functions\n$1,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x),…$\nare orthogonal with respect to the defined inner product, the only nonzero term in the final expression above is\n\n$$\n{b}_{3}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)⟩.\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)⟩ & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{2}x-\\frac{1}{12}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)]}_{-\\pi }^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\pi -(-\\pi ))-\\frac{1}{12}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\pi )-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-6\\pi ))) \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)⟩ & ={b}_{3}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)⟩ \\\\ {b}_{3} & =\\frac{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)⟩}{⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)⟩} \\\\ {b}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\cdot {\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }({x}^{2}+6x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186330854 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "304c015d-cbb6-5989-a102-d6f0f5f38604" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Expressions For Fourier Coefficients"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f0cd75e5-88f5-50f5-89c8-b5a937eb2863" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩}{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)⟩}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330856} {:answer/id #uuid "7321acdf-6727-520a-8c15-679cee4a0787" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)⟩}{⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330857} {:answer/id #uuid "f813cd98-2b20-5ebb-b8ae-a99e3ee7b11b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)⟩}{⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)⟩}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330858} {:answer/id #uuid "4117ec21-c98e-5ec0-b36a-8dd5607c1f1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩}{⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330859} {:answer/id #uuid "63f3b34b-8410-592b-b08d-1883d0f24568" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)⟩}{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)⟩}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330860}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f813cd98-2b20-5ebb-b8ae-a99e3ee7b11b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)⟩}{⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)⟩}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330858} :answer-field/id #uuid "83fb5c49-6edc-5a23-a18f-29528bc423e7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330855} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08be0311-b8a9-5c52-ad43-dcde04095648" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330862} {:answer/id #uuid "03efb26a-2460-5688-86ad-aa05eb5e5765" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330863} {:answer/id #uuid "1364baf6-662d-5d56-bde6-911b4d39aa95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330864} {:answer/id #uuid "3962afd9-1242-5cab-946c-a3e01d2c8951" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330865} {:answer/id #uuid "690cdf9c-5471-5db4-bc90-d43619deb44d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330866}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "690cdf9c-5471-5db4-bc90-d43619deb44d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330866} :answer-field/id #uuid "c414ab39-7de6-593a-8f44-518bde5d8c4c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330861} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03b6037f-9cd6-52f3-b957-917eb611f528" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }({x}^{2}-6x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330868} {:answer/id #uuid "f16beace-a78a-510c-84c4-77bb7ca374f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }({x}^{2}-6x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330869} {:answer/id #uuid "a54c133c-962b-5568-a016-f0bac9e0bda2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }({x}^{2}-6x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330870} {:answer/id #uuid "405674ed-13de-581d-b036-81306aef7df5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }({x}^{2}-6x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330871} {:answer/id #uuid "8a242fbd-275b-577f-9536-c10c4c6d7a8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }({x}^{2}-6x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330872} {:answer/id #uuid "e1908470-9458-52ad-a8a0-d2c6b538ba11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }({x}^{2}-6x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330873}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8a242fbd-275b-577f-9536-c10c4c6d7a8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }({x}^{2}-6x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330872} :answer-field/id #uuid "549fff5d-30f8-53a7-bc0c-1f6faf082c6c" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330867}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "02ca39fb-07ed-50d7-9c80-f756da5d6843" :question/math-academy-id "q-340662" :question/problem "Let $F(x)={x}^{2}-6x$ for $x\\in R,$ and let $f(x)$ be the $2\\pi $-periodic extension of $F$ from $x\\in [0,2\\pi )$ to $x\\in R.$ The Fourier series of $f$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the expression for the coefficient ${b}_{4}$ below, where $⟨\\,,⟩$ denotes the usual inner product on functions over $[0,2\\pi ).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{4} & ={{field-1}} \\\\  & ={{field-2}}\\,\\cdot \\,{{field-3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The usual inner product of functions $f$ and $g$ over $x\\in [0,2\\pi )$ is given by\n\n$$\n⟨f(x),g(x)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(x)g(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFor a $2\\pi $-periodic function $f(x)$ that's piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,2\\pi ),$ the Fourier series of $f$ is given by \n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)),\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{0},$ ${a}_{n},$ and ${b}_{n}$ are constants to be determined. \n\nNow, we find the inner product of $f(x)$ with $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)⟩ & = \\\\ ⟨\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)⟩ & = \\\\ ⟨\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2},\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }⟨{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }⟨{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)⟩ & = \\\\ \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}⟨1,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the functions\n$1,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x),…$\nare orthogonal with respect to the defined inner product, the only nonzero term in the final expression above is\n\n$$\n{b}_{4}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)⟩.\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{2}x-\\frac{1}{16}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8x)]}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2\\pi -0)-\\frac{1}{16}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(16\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)⟩ & ={b}_{4}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)⟩ \\\\ {b}_{4} & =\\frac{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)⟩}{⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)⟩} \\\\ {b}_{4} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }({x}^{2}-6x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186330874 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fadf4f64-3569-56b2-98e1-f6681892054a" :knowledge-point/title "Checking Orthogonality of Sines and Cosines"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5e01dd4e-809b-51e0-9a43-462183c2c10e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330876} {:answer/id #uuid "351a7b47-b976-5e7e-8338-43dedc9e07f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330877} {:answer/id #uuid "fa0b26cf-59c1-5be7-8143-094a25436d90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330878} {:answer/id #uuid "6d71a200-4fde-56e3-89eb-612366eaa5da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330879}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fa0b26cf-59c1-5be7-8143-094a25436d90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330878} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb2a7f91-0299-5732-a8d7-47afde96f706" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330875} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01ae4e44-8fa9-59ce-9062-7729414087dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "are orthogonal" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330881} {:answer/id #uuid "f3522545-009b-57b0-ae0e-aaaccbb5a0ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "are not orthogonal" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330882}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "01ae4e44-8fa9-59ce-9062-7729414087dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "are orthogonal" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330881} :answer-field/id #uuid "2c493f88-f9cc-5bb8-8cd0-9c7b19d5d99e" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330880}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3d6ca311-b229-51f5-b6ee-b805697465fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-340669" :question/problem "$$\n⟨f(x),g(x)⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)g(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nGiven the inner product on the set of continuous functions over $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ]$ defined above, select the correct options below.$\\,$\n$⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x),1⟩=$ \n{{field-1}}\n\n$\\,$\nThe functions $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)$ and $g(x)=1$ \n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Evaluating the inner product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x),1⟩ & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)]}_{-\\pi }^{\\pi } \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{6}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-6\\pi )) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{6}(1-1) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)$ and $g(x)=1$ $\\text{are orthogonal}$ with respect to the inner product."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186330883 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fadf4f64-3569-56b2-98e1-f6681892054a" :knowledge-point/title "Checking Orthogonality of Sines and Cosines"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4185591d-cf23-56fb-97ac-67cc6af5b0c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330885} {:answer/id #uuid "3cc09566-4118-5f8c-bcff-234281fc3454" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330886} {:answer/id #uuid "b3c8a793-084e-5bd6-a628-7e9f077561c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330887} {:answer/id #uuid "a978ddc4-0dc7-5677-b7aa-e220660ce360" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330888} {:answer/id #uuid "09e82fd0-7a50-549b-8941-0da3ea12f453" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330889}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a978ddc4-0dc7-5677-b7aa-e220660ce360" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330888} :answer-field/id #uuid "c6b9df2f-46a6-5e16-92ef-d7113796a1f4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330884} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00a2052a-e6ff-59d0-ae43-cb395dbc1a9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "are orthogonal" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330891} {:answer/id #uuid "cfb41ff8-dbf9-5ce7-8f0d-6c448e4d0de2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "are not orthogonal" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330892}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "00a2052a-e6ff-59d0-ae43-cb395dbc1a9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "are orthogonal" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330891} :answer-field/id #uuid "d0fff300-ca8f-53e6-b368-c3f63f82b9a2" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330890}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e05d9234-b5df-5b04-8082-fca6a02fdae1" :question/math-academy-id "q-340677" :question/problem "$$\n⟨f(x),g(x)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(x)g(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nGiven the inner product on the set of continuous functions over $x\\in [0,2\\pi )$ defined above, select the correct options below.$\\,$\n$⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)⟩=$\n{{field-1}}\n\n$\\,$\nThe functions $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)$ and $g(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)$ \n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "We begin by using the product-to-sum identity\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)=\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x)).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)-\\frac{1}{16}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8x)]}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0))-\\frac{1}{16}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(16\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(0-0)-\\frac{1}{16}(0-0) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)$ and $g(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)$ $\\text{are orthogonal}$ with respect to the inner product."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186330893 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fadf4f64-3569-56b2-98e1-f6681892054a" :knowledge-point/title "Checking Orthogonality of Sines and Cosines"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a591a9c5-79b5-5a05-92f6-6dea78bd0af1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330895} {:answer/id #uuid "6d1b83a2-fdfe-5286-be19-4d51bfbd2fc8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330896} {:answer/id #uuid "a0ddfc34-1cc0-505c-beb8-339303252840" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330897} {:answer/id #uuid "2d5f7b38-6927-5b41-8601-8469f16fa008" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330898} {:answer/id #uuid "41f06080-a04c-50f0-9aac-dec35eea4428" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330899}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2d5f7b38-6927-5b41-8601-8469f16fa008" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330898} :answer-field/id #uuid "0fd06ea0-0027-5d72-bdf0-28d570b345bb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330894} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c425e2bd-dec6-5f78-9f5e-719adb4bee81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "are orthogonal" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330901} {:answer/id #uuid "3f7ce501-9fd7-58ce-818c-2aaa7e695a67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "are not orthogonal" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330902}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c425e2bd-dec6-5f78-9f5e-719adb4bee81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "are orthogonal" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330901} :answer-field/id #uuid "f0ad69b5-4fef-5aa5-82fd-35456d480c40" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330900}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "bf13831d-c995-5cd8-9588-b47dc713d664" :question/math-academy-id "q-340681" :question/problem "$$\n⟨f(x),g(x)⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)g(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nGiven the inner product on the set of continuous functions over $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi )$ defined above, select the correct options below.$\\,$\n$⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)⟩=$\n{{field-1}}\n\n$\\,$\nThe functions $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)$ and $g(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)$ \n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "We begin by using the product-to-sum identity\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)=\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1x)).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)⟩ & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7x)+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{14}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7x)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)]}_{-\\pi }^{\\pi } \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{14}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-7\\pi ))-\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-\\pi )) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{14}(-1-(-1))-\\frac{1}{2}(-1-(-1)) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)$ and $g(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)$ $\\text{are orthogonal}$ with respect to the inner product."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186330903 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "544f5154-5213-5898-a45d-4546bc8004c8" :knowledge-point/title "Understanding the Periodic Extension of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "108fcc22-ca64-5246-9b40-9a748070b495" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330905} {:answer/id #uuid "3c3f6972-0ba2-5dde-959d-edc542565a21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330906} {:answer/id #uuid "559e4c24-e781-5507-b95e-c4a3c7fee94d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330907} {:answer/id #uuid "23b0f7c0-ed71-57b8-b08d-389399a65c1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330908} {:answer/id #uuid "9e7430a2-9497-5027-8b35-cc669e965194" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330909}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3c3f6972-0ba2-5dde-959d-edc542565a21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330906} :answer-field/id #uuid "d3a68ebd-0432-5044-a77d-d9e7a454a701" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330904}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "33678672-599f-5ed7-bc71-566489deab66" :question/math-academy-id "q-341232" :question/problem "Let $F(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$ for $x\\in R.$ Suppose $f(x)$ is the $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ from $x\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ to $x\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R$\n- $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in R$\n- $f(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})=f(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ for all $x\\in R$" :question/worked-solution "Since $f$ is the $\\pi /2$-periodic extension of $F,$ we have  \n\n- $f(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R,$ and\n\n- $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\nWith that in mind, we examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. This is exactly the definition of $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$-periodicity:\n\n$$\nf(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})=f(x)\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in R.$\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, take $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.$ Using the periodicity of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})=f(\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{\\pi }{2})=f(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=F(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})=1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{3\\pi }{4})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x)\\ne F(x)$ for $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $f$ is $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$-periodic, any shift by a multiple of its period returns the same value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})=f(x)\\,\\text{and}\\,f(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})=f((x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\frac{\\pi }{2})=f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince both sides equal $f(x),$ the statement is true for all $x\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186330910 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "544f5154-5213-5898-a45d-4546bc8004c8" :knowledge-point/title "Understanding the Periodic Extension of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "50da47be-a0ba-557d-bade-5daf37744529" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330912} {:answer/id #uuid "f801b0bd-2625-592b-b995-d97db5cacc07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330913} {:answer/id #uuid "394ace35-2c4d-51dd-a3c8-3c9583ae58e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330914} {:answer/id #uuid "ac29b32f-dcdf-5d97-a08c-c3298ede211f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330915} {:answer/id #uuid "ca4a9674-21d6-5a3b-b74a-078119552862" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330916}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f801b0bd-2625-592b-b995-d97db5cacc07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330913} :answer-field/id #uuid "1c115180-46e9-59de-95cf-762152a976ec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330911}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0e9ccb5a-d413-5585-be40-3136be85f39e" :question/math-academy-id "q-341237" :question/problem "Let $F(x)=cos\\,(\\frac{x}{2})$ for $x\\in R.$ Suppose $f(x)$ is the $2\\pi $-periodic extension of $F(x)$ from $x\\in [0,2\\pi )$ to $x\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x+4\\pi )=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R$\n- $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in R$\n- $f(x+2\\pi )=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R$" :question/worked-solution "Since $f$ is the $2\\pi $-periodic extension of $F,$ we have  \n\n- $f(x+2\\pi )=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R,$ and\n\n- $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in [0,2\\pi ).$\n\nWith that in mind, we examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have\n\n$$\nf(x+4\\pi )=f((x+2\\pi )+2\\pi )=f(x+2\\pi )=f(x)\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in R.$\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, take $x=2\\pi .$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2\\pi ) & =f(0+2\\pi )=f(0)=F(0)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)=1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\nF(2\\pi )=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )=-1.\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x)\\ne F(x)$ for $x=2\\pi .$\n\n- Statement III is true. This is exactly the definition of $2\\pi $-periodicity: \n\n$$\nf(x+2\\pi )=f(x)\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"."}]}
